    "lawCode": "Amd §3218, CPLR",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3851A\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to prohib-\niting certain judgments by confession; and prohibiting certain financial\ninstitutions from using a marshal to execute certain judgments\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would prohibit certain judgments by confession; and prohibits\ncertain financial institutions from using a marshal to execute certain\njudgments.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n \nSECTION 1: Prohibits certain \"confession of judgments.\"  No judgment\nby confession may be entered on: (1) any amount due from one or more\nindividuals for personal, family, household, consumer, investment, or\nnon-business purposes; (2) any amount under $250,000 due from any person\nfor any purpose; or (3) any amount due from a person that:  (i.) is\ncurrently a nonresident of the state, (ii.) was a nonresident of the\nstate at the time the affidavit authorizing entry of the judgment by\nconfession was executed, or; (iii.) is not an individual, does not oper-\nate a place of business in the state, or did not operate a place of\nbusiness in the state at the time the affidavit authorizing entry of the\njudgment by confession was executed.\n\n \nSECTION 2: Notwithstanding any provision of law to the contrary,\nfinancial institutions that offer any financial product or service shall\nnot use a marshal, as allowed for in paragraph (b) of section 1609 of\nthe New York civil court act, for purposes of executing any money judg-\nment relating to a confession of judgement.\n\n \nSECTION 3: Effective date.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNo prior legislative history.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNo fiscal implications.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
